metric_definitions.revised:
{\color{blue}Point prediction is evaluated by root mean squared error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), and $R^2=1-\sum_i(y_i-\hat y_i)^2/\sum_i(y_i-\bar y)^2$. Test $R^2$ can be negative when prediction error exceeds that of the test-mean reference. For a quantile forecast $q_\tau$ and residual $r=y-q_\tau$, the pinball loss is $\rho_\tau(r)=r\{\tau-\mathbf{1}(r<0)\}$. We report its mean at each of nine quantiles and their unweighted average. This aggregate remains defined when independently fitted quantiles cross. Reliability at level $\tau$ is summarized by the empirical cumulative probability $\widehat p_\tau=n^{-1}\sum_i\mathbf{1}\{y_i\le q_\tau(x_i)\}$ and the mean absolute calibration error $J^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^J|\widehat p_{\tau_j}-\tau_j|$. The quantile-crossing rate is the fraction of rows having any adjacent decrease.}

{\color{blue}For a central interval $[\ell,u]$ with miscoverage $\alpha$, the interval score is}
\begin{equation}
{\color{blue}
\operatorname{IS}_{\alpha}(y,\ell,u)=(u-\ell)+\frac{2}{\alpha}(\ell-y)\mathbf{1}\{y<\ell\}+\frac{2}{\alpha}(y-u)\mathbf{1}\{y>u\}.}
\end{equation}
{\color{blue}Its sample mean is the Winkler score. Prediction interval coverage probability (PICP) is the covered fraction and mean prediction interval width (MPIW) is $n^{-1}\sum_i(u_i-\ell_i)$. For $K=4$ central intervals with $\alpha_k\in\{0.50,0.20,0.10,0.05\}$ and median $m$, the weighted interval score is}
\begin{equation}
{\color{blue}
\operatorname{WIS}=\frac{0.5|y-m|+\sum_{k=1}^{K}(\alpha_k/2)\operatorname{IS}_{\alpha_k}}{K+0.5}.}
\end{equation}
{\color{blue}Nine independently fitted quantiles are retained for the raw pinball and crossing diagnostics, then sorted within each forecast row by a fixed label-free monotone rearrangement \citep{chernozhukov2010rearrangement} before direct-quantile WIS and nested-interval evaluation. Raw and rearranged reliability curves are both reported; the score table retains raw mean pinball loss and crossing together with rearranged WIS and calibration error. The Q2-expanded intervals are evaluated separately through PICP, MPIW, and Winkler score and are not used to compute WIS. Width, interval score, and WIS are expressed in MW; lower values are preferable when interpreted together with reliability. The new primary evaluation does not use the coverage--width criterion (CWC), whose thresholded penalty and normalization depend on user-selected constants; it instead gives PICP and MPIW separately and uses the proper interval and weighted interval scores. Where reported for historical comparisons, PINAW divides mean width by the observed test-label range rather than farm capacity.}

chronological_protocol.revised:
{\color{blue}The experiments use the supplied original workbooks with chronological separation of fitting, validation, calibration, and testing. Model fitting uses 1 January--31 December 2019. The first quarter of 2020 is the validation period; the common-setting analyses below do not use it for parameter selection, while the separate validation-selected comparison specifies its selection procedure explicitly; April--June 2020 supplies interval calibration labels, and July--December 2020 supplies test labels. All boundaries are left-inclusive and right-exclusive at midnight. The forecast horizon is 15 minutes: each input contains the preceding 24 observations, with consecutive input-to-input and last-input-to-target intervals of exactly 15 minutes. Adjacent windows on opposite sides of a period boundary may share already observed input rows, as in rolling forecasting, but every forecast target belongs to exactly one period. No Q1, Q2, or Q3--Q4 label enters the 2019 fit; no Q2 or Q3--Q4 label enters Q1 model selection; and no Q3--Q4 label enters fixed Q2 calibration. Causal rolling calibration is evaluated separately and adds an earlier test outcome only after issuing its forecast. Random sampling is confined to the 2019 fitting pool and to algorithmic landmark seeds; it does not create the calendar partitions.}

{\color{blue}The input schema common to all six sites comprises four wind speeds, temperature, and sine/cosine encodings of four wind directions, giving 13 features per time step and 312 flattened inputs. Times written as 24:00 are normalized to the next day; all rows sharing an ambiguous duplicate timestamp are removed before chronological sorting. Missing codes ($-99$), nonfinite values, all-zero meteorological records, negative wind speeds, and directions outside $[0,360]$ are treated as missing covariates. Targets with missing power, hub wind, or temperature and windows with non-15-minute gaps are excluded by fixed rules. Other power values, including negative or above-capacity observations, are retained. Missing input values are replaced by lag-specific fitting means; an entirely missing coordinate uses zero. Feature standardization and capacity-normalized label standardization use only the observations actually fitted in that branch, excluding unused target labels in scarcity experiments. No future-value filling, clipping, or error-based exclusions are applied. The original files are preserved, although their upstream measurement and processing history is not fully documented.}

{\color{blue}Domain thresholds use valid meteorological observations from 2019 only. Farm~6 thresholds are 15.43\,m/s for wind and 10.24/31.36\,$^\circ$C for temperature; its training set contains 32,832 source and 2,076 target windows. Table~\ref{tab:chronological_counts} reports all six sites. Test membership is determined retrospectively from observed target-time weather; this protocol does not forecast event onset. The time split, missing-value rules, and common-setting comparison grids were specified before those fits. This is a temporal holdout within a previously studied public dataset.}

chronological_settings.revised:
{\color{blue}The common-setting component and sensitivity comparisons fix $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=1$, RBF bandwidth parameter $\gamma=0.003$, 256 requested landmarks, batch size 256, Adam learning rate 0.01, and 64 epochs, on an Intel Xeon Platinum 8452Y host with one computational thread (Python 3.10.9, NumPy 2.2.6, and scikit-learn 1.7.2). These settings define a common reference configuration; validation-based model selection is reported separately. Unless varied explicitly, loss coefficients are $C_S=1000/(2n_S)$ and $C_T=1000/(2n_T)$, so both domains receive equal total coefficient mass and $n_SC_S+n_TC_T=1000$. Target-only fitting uses $C_T=1000/n_T$, centered target residual variance, no projected discrepancy, and its own fitting statistics and landmarks. Its epochs are raised to the smallest integer giving at least as many Adam updates as the paired full model; equal update opportunities do not certify equal optimization accuracy. The full, no-variance, no-discrepancy, and target-only variants use seeds 0, 1, and 2. These are algorithmic repeats on a shared test period, not independent datasets or confidence intervals.}

{\color{blue}Point prediction uses $\tau=0.5$. Interval fits use $\tau_L=0.025$ and $\tau_U=0.975$. Define ordered endpoints $\ell(x)=\min\{f_{\tau_L}(x),f_{\tau_U}(x)\}$ and $u(x)=\max\{f_{\tau_L}(x),f_{\tau_U}(x)\}$. Calibration scores are $s_i=\max\{\ell(x_i)-y_i,y_i-u(x_i),0\}$. With $\alpha=0.05$, set $k=\lceil(n_{\rm cal}+1)(1-\alpha)\rceil$, $q=s_{(k)}$, and report $[\ell(x)-q,u(x)+q]$. A finite interval requires $k\le n_{\rm cal}$. On Farm~6, $n_{\rm cal}=225$ and $k=215$. Test labels do not set the expansion. This is an empirical temporal evaluation of a split-calibration rule, without an exchangeability or conditional-coverage guarantee \citep{romano2019cqr}.}

{\color{blue}Each branch saves source/target fitting identifiers, preprocessing statistics, landmarks, model coefficients, calibration scores, and individual test predictions. Code and workbook hashes accompany the fixed protocol. Reported fitting time includes landmark selection, feature construction, regularizer construction, and Adam, but excludes workbook loading, preprocessing, prediction, and post-fit objective diagnostics. Timings are wall-clock measurements from a shared compute host. Seed ranges throughout these analyses describe algorithmic variation rather than confidence intervals; fitting times do not represent inference or deployment latency.}

