solver_accuracy.revised:
\subsection{Optimization accuracy for fixed features}\label{sec:solver_accuracy}
{\color{blue}To monitor optimization accuracy for each fixed-feature fit, we also solve Eq.~\eqref{eq:nystrom_primal} by the alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) \citep{boyd2011admm}. Let $\boldsymbol{Z}=[\boldsymbol{\Psi}_S^T,\boldsymbol{\Psi}_T^T]^T$ and introduce residuals $\boldsymbol{e}=\boldsymbol{y}-\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{w}-b\mathbf1$. The quadratic weight/intercept update eliminates the unregularized intercept by centering $\boldsymbol{Z}$, then solves an $m\times m$ system with matrix $\boldsymbol{R}+\rho\boldsymbol{Z}_c^T\boldsymbol{Z}_c$. The residual update is elementwise asymmetric soft thresholding, with positive and negative thresholds $C_i\tau/\rho$ and $C_i(1-\tau)/\rho$. The penalty parameter $\rho$ is adjusted using training residuals only.}

{\color{blue}A feasible dual vector supplies a numerical lower bound on the fixed-feature optimum. With the same label-only constant omitted as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:nystrom_primal}, the dual is}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:feature_dual_bound}
{\color{blue}\begin{aligned}
\max_{\boldsymbol{v}}\quad D(\boldsymbol{v})
&=\boldsymbol{y}^T\boldsymbol{v}-\frac12(\boldsymbol{c}+\boldsymbol{Z}^T\boldsymbol{v})^T
\boldsymbol{R}^{-1}(\boldsymbol{c}+\boldsymbol{Z}^T\boldsymbol{v}),\\
\text{s.t.}\quad \mathbf1^T\boldsymbol{v}&=0,\qquad C_i(\tau-1)\le v_i\le C_i\tau.
\end{aligned}}
\end{equation}
{\color{blue}This follows by minimizing the Lagrangian over $\boldsymbol{w}$ and $b$ and applying the conjugate of the weighted pinball loss. Projecting the ADMM multiplier onto the box and zero-sum constraints gives a feasible dual iterate. For the best retained primal and dual values, $P-D$ bounds training suboptimality up to numerical feasibility error. The stopping rule is $P-D\le10^{-7}+10^{-3}\max\{1,|P|,|D|\}$, checked every 25 iterations, with a limit of 3,000 iterations. The absolute gap is unchanged by the omitted label-only constant; the relative normalization refers specifically to the displayed objective. Every run retains its gap, feasibility residuals, and stopping status, including runs reaching the iteration limit. This check concerns optimization of the chosen feature representation, not approximation error relative to the exact kernel or predictive generalization.}

{\color{blue}After constructing $\boldsymbol{Z}_c^T\boldsymbol{Z}_c$, each ADMM iteration costs $O(Nm+m^2)$; a change of $\rho$ requires another $O(m^3)$ factorization. Diagnostic checks additionally evaluate the training objective and feasible dual bound, optimize the intercept by a weighted-quantile sort, and project the dual vector. Their cost is $O(Nm+m^2+N\log N)$ with a fixed cap on the scalar projection iterations. Small problems at three quantiles, including rank-deficient features and unequal or zero sample weights, are checked against an independently formulated primal quadratic program.}

selection_protocol.revised:
\subsection{Validation-selected predictive comparison}\label{sec:selection}
{\color{blue}We compare TL-QLDMR, its no-variance and no-discrepancy variants, weighted support vector quantile regression (SVQR), target-only SVQR, and histogram gradient boosting with quantile loss (HistGBR) in a common Python~3.10.9 runner. The five kernel methods use the reported NumPy~2.2.6 fixed-Nystr\"{o}m and ADMM implementation, whereas HistGBR uses scikit-learn~1.7.2's \texttt{HistGradientBoostingRegressor} with quantile loss \citep{sklearn17hist}. Weighted SVQR sets $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=0$ in the same pooled fitting procedure; target-only SVQR uses the selected target fitting labels alone and has $R=I$, $c=0$, with no residual-variance term. It therefore differs from the target-only residual-variance model in the common-setting analysis. Every method receives the same meteorological input schema and target validation, calibration, and test windows. Transfer methods and HistGBR share the source and target fitting observations. Within Farm~6, 7,500 temporally spaced source windows and all 2,076 target fitting labels are used. For cross-farm adaptation, each target site receives 1,500 normal-domain source windows from each of the other five sites and 128 target fitting labels. These 128 labels are stratified using training-period month and disjoint weather category (cold, then hot, then high wind). Every nonempty stratum receives one observation; the remaining quota is allocated proportionally to its remaining size, with temporal spacing within strata. This preserves rare training conditions without selecting examples by prediction error.}

{\color{blue}Each method receives 16 candidate configurations, selected using target-domain Q1 RMSE for $\tau=0.5$. The five kernel methods share one reference configuration and a 15-point Latin hypercube design, with $\gamma\in[10^{-4},10^{-1}]$, total loss coefficient $A\in[10^2,10^5]$, target mass fraction $p\in[0.05,0.95]$, and positive penalty coefficients in $[10^{-2},10^3]$. Except for $p$, these coordinates are sampled on logarithmic scales. Source and target coefficients are $C_S=A(1-p)/n_S$ and $C_T=Ap/n_T$; removed penalties are set to zero, and target-only fitting uses $C_T=A/n_T$. The reference is $(\gamma,A,p,\lambda_1,\lambda_2)=(0.003,1000,0.5,1,1)$. HistGBR receives 16 configurations sampled without replacement from learning rates $\{0.03,0.1\}$, leaf limits $\{7,15,31\}$, minimum leaf counts $\{10,20,40\}$, $L_2$ weights $\{0,1,10\}$, iteration counts $\{100,200\}$, and target mass fractions $\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}$. Its sample weights have mean one; internal early stopping is disabled. Equal candidate counts specify the search budget, not equal computational cost or exhaustive optimization.}

{\color{blue}Kernel models use 128 requested landmarks, fitting-only preprocessing and landmark pools, and the ADMM stopping rule in Section~\ref{sec:solver_accuracy}. All 42 method--task selections use seed 0 and are locked before generating the follow-up Q3--Q4 forecasts. Selected kernel settings are repeated with landmark seeds 0, 1, and 2 on unchanged fitting labels; deterministic HistGBR is evaluated once. The point-selected settings are reused for quantiles 0.025 and 0.975, so interval parameters are not separately optimized. Validation labels are not added to the final fitting set. Cross-farm supervision therefore comprises 128 fitting labels plus respectively 169, 617, 2,410, 212, 134, and 177 validation labels, and 305, 102, 862, 319, 362, and 225 calibration labels for sites 1--6. The calendar split is unchanged from the common-setting study. Because its test results were already examined before designing this follow-up, the new comparison is validation-selected evidence on a reused test period, rather than an independent confirmation dataset.}

