related_wind_forecasting.revised:
\subsection{Wind forecasting under variability and uncertainty}
{\color{blue}Hybrid signal-processing models explicitly separate temporal scales before prediction. For example, \citet{abedinia2022multilevel} combine wavelet decomposition, entropy-based feature selection, and SVM for short-term wind forecasting. This construction can improve a point predictor by filtering and selecting multiscale components, but it neither estimates a predictive distribution nor studies supervised transfer from normal to rare operating conditions. Deep architectures instead learn temporal representations: recent spatio-temporal and diffusion models increase expressive capacity for wind-power forecasting \citep{liu2025diffusion,zhang2026gwsstnet}, while transfer networks reuse representations across wind-power domains \citep{yin2021serioparallel,dong2023mdan}. Their flexibility is useful for complex temporal structure, but model capacity, training data, optimization, and tuning protocol remain part of the empirical comparison rather than an automatic advantage.}

{\color{blue}Probabilistic work has moved beyond a single fixed interval. \citet{abedinia2024quantile} estimate a wind-power quantile function with a CNN--LSTM and optimize a CRPS-based criterion. \citet{serttas2026multiscale} combine adaptive variational mode decomposition, a dual-stream CNN--BiLSTM, quantile outputs, and SHAP explanations for regional wind-speed forecasting. \citet{yu2025ramping} use CNN--LSTM quantile prediction for uncertain net-load ramping demand. The latter two tasks concern wind speed and system ramping demand, respectively, rather than cross-domain extreme-condition wind-power output. Conformalized regression forests and related methods add marginal coverage control to regional wind-power intervals \citep{jonkers2024regional,romano2019cqr}, although temporal dependence and distribution shift require assumptions beyond the nominal calibration formula.}

{\color{blue}These strands motivate a narrower role for the present kernel formulation. Fixed kernel or Nystr\"{o}m features permit a convex quantile objective and numerical primal--dual checks, which is useful when target labels are scarce. They do not learn multiscale or spatial representations automatically, and their performance depends on the kernel, rank, regularization, and source--target relation. We therefore compare against both kernel and tree alternatives and treat deep representation learning as a complementary approach rather than claim general superiority for kernel regression. Convexity and a fixed feature map make the optimization objective auditable, but do not by themselves make input effects interpretable. We do not present feature attributions, input-sensitivity maps, physical-consistency constraints, or operational explanation examples, and therefore make no empirical interpretability claim.}

selection_point_inference.revised:
\subsubsection{Paired temporal-block inference}
{\color{blue}The same test observations make method errors paired, while adjacent meteorological-tail forecasts remain serially dependent. We therefore average squared error across landmark seeds within each forecast, partition the Q3--Q4 timestamps into non-overlapping seven-day calendar blocks, and estimate the Full-minus-comparator mean squared-error difference. We use 10,000 block-bootstrap replicates for 95\% intervals and 99,999 two-sided block sign flips, followed by Holm adjustment over the 18 comparisons with weighted SVQR, target-only SVQR, and HistGBR across six farms. The block sums and counts give the same observation-weighted estimand in the effect, bootstrap, and test. The bootstrap treats block effects as approximately independent and exchangeable; the sign-flip test additionally requires approximate sign symmetry under the null. Landmark seeds are algorithmic variation rather than independent replicates.}

{\color{blue}After Holm adjustment, the full model has lower squared error than target-only SVQR on Farm~2 (difference $-168.454$\,MW$^2$, 95\% block-bootstrap interval $[-377.132,-54.139]$, $p_{\rm H}=0.033$), but higher squared error than HistGBR on Farm~1 ($104.912$\,MW$^2$, $[57.347,169.957]$, $p_{\rm H}=0.033$) and Farm~5 ($113.828$\,MW$^2$, $[82.542,142.345]$, $p_{\rm H}<0.001$). None of the other 15 comparisons reaches 0.05 after multiplicity adjustment. Table~\ref{tab:selected_point_inference} retains all tests. The analysis is exploratory because it uses a previously examined test period and only 10--26 temporal blocks per site; the block assumption does not create independent events or an independent confirmation dataset.}

probabilistic_multiquantile.revised:
\subsubsection{Multi-quantile calibration and proper scores}
{\color{blue}A separate probability-specific comparison uses Farm~6 and the fixed quantile grid}
\begin{equation*}
{\color{blue}\mathcal{T}=\{0.025,0.05,0.10,0.25,0.50,0.75,0.90,0.95,0.975\}.}
\end{equation*}
{\color{blue}Each of the six methods receives the same 16-candidate tuning budget. The five kernel methods share the candidate lists defined above, with inactive coordinates ignored, while HistGBR uses its stated 16-configuration draw from the tree-specific grid. A candidate is scored by unweighted mean pinball loss over all nine Q1 quantiles; ties use its fixed index, and convergence status does not filter candidates. The 96 method--candidate records, comprising 864 seed-0 quantile fits, are completed and the six selections are locked before any Q2 calibration or Q3--Q4 prediction. Models continue to use 2019 fitting rows only. Selected kernel settings are then evaluated with landmark seeds 0, 1, and 2, while deterministic HistGBR uses seed 0. Thus equal candidate counts define a common probability-selection budget without implying equal model capacity or computational cost. The locked configurations are reported in Table~\ref{tab:probabilistic_configs}.}

{\color{blue}Raw forecasts are retained for quantile-specific pinball loss, empirical reliability, and crossing diagnostics. A fixed label-free monotone rearrangement sorts the nine forecasts within each row before nested intervals and WIS are computed. Q2 scores separately calibrate 50\%, 80\%, 90\%, and 95\% central intervals; the same fixed and causal rolling rules are applied to every method at every level. No test label selects a configuration, rearrangement, or fixed expansion. Because the test period had been examined before this analysis was designed, the results remain exploratory reused-test evidence.}

{\color{blue}Table~\ref{tab:probabilistic_scores} shows that HistGBR has the lowest raw nine-quantile mean pinball loss (1.767\,MW) and rearranged WIS (3.531\,MW), followed by target-only SVQR (1.995 and 3.971\,MW). The full model gives 2.045 and 4.090\,MW. Its raw crossing rate is zero, as is that of the no-discrepancy variant, whereas the other four methods cross on 38.07\%--45.84\% of test rows. This ordering stability does not translate into the lowest proper score or calibration error: the full-model rearranged reliability MACE is 8.66 percentage points, compared with 7.35 for the no-variance and weighted-SVQR fits. These results do not establish a general probabilistic advantage for the complete objective.}

{\color{blue}For the full model, fixed Q2 calibration gives PICP values 0.532, 0.894, 0.960, and 0.977 at nominal coverages 0.50, 0.80, 0.90, and 0.95, with widths 11.21, 30.86, 41.52, and 54.75\,MW (Table~\ref{tab:probabilistic_coverage}). Figure~\ref{fig:probabilistic_reliability} reports all nine reliability points before and after rearrangement, while Fig.~\ref{fig:probabilistic_coverage} shows the static coverage--width trade-off without selecting methods or levels by test performance. Causal rolling results are retained in the table as a separate information setting.}

